```java
class Vehicle {
    void stop() {
        System.out.println("Engine off");
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    @Override
    void stop() {
        super.stop();               // run Vehicle's shared behavior first
        System.out.println("Retracting antenna");
    }
}

Vehicle v = new Car();
v.stop();
// prints:
// Engine off
// Retracting antenna
```

The override adds Car-specific behavior on top of Vehicle's, rather than replacing it — dropping `super.stop()` would silently lose the engine-off step. Note that `v.stop()` runs `Car`'s override even though `v` is declared as `Vehicle`, because dispatch follows the object's actual type.
